# A table's w:jc places it across the text column (#1922): `center`
# centres it, `right` puts it at the right edge, none leaves it at the
# left, and a centred table wider than the column stays at the column's
# left (its side margins come to 0) and scrolls sideways (#1895). `doc`'s
# `flow.tables.N.place` reads each top-level table's painted edges
# against the text column it is drawn in: `left`, `center`, `right` or
# `other`. The fixture's tables are fixed (dxa), so Web Layout keeps
# their widths. Each read follows a `shot`, which waits for the frame
# after the change.

test issue 1922 a table is drawn where its w:jc puts it, in Web and Print Layout
  open copy:../fixtures/table-jc.docx
  assert view.layout is web
  call window-size {"w":1100,"h":900}
  shot window
  call doc {}
  assert reply.flow.tables.0.width is 200
  assert reply.flow.tables.0.place is left
  assert reply.flow.tables.1.width is 200
  assert reply.flow.tables.1.place is center
  assert reply.flow.tables.2.place is right
  # Wider than the view: at the text's left, as wide as its grid, and the
  # view scrolls sideways to it.
  assert reply.flow.tables.3.width is 2000
  assert reply.flow.tables.3.left is {doc.flow.tables.0.left}
  assert reply.flow.max_scroll_x is not 0
  # Print Layout: the page's text column.
  call ribbon-click {"tab":"View","command":"Print Layout"}
  assert view.layout is print
  shot window
  call doc {}
  assert reply.flow.tables.0.place is left
  assert reply.flow.tables.1.place is center
  assert reply.flow.tables.2.place is right
  assert reply.flow.tables.3.left is {doc.flow.tables.0.left}
